[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug with one fix queued. The reports say otherwise: two
problems, two fixes. Each one now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes the first problem only. The other one stays open after it ships.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

- **`parser`:** the shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's
  7 April arrives as 4 July. Ticket: [parser reads day-first dates month first](https://github.com/MonikaPerendyk/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
- **`hint`:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint
  says and the form reads it the tenant's way. The parser fix does not touch this.
  Ticket: [booking-v3 date hint is month first](https://github.com/MonikaPerendyk/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
- Five reports the table footnotes as a one-day move stay under `parser`, as the desk files
  them. Their dates move by one day rather than flip, so the parser ticket asks for a time-zone
  check when the fix is verified.

The report ids for each problem are in its ticket.

## Why one ticket hid two

- The desk merges on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  Every subject in #4471 appears under more than one cause, so the rule merged them all.
- Intake tags are a guess from the reporter's browser. They disagree with the cause rules in
  both directions; the table above shows them.
- The one-day reports are counted under `parser` and footnoted; the parser ticket carries them.
- The export holds duplicates from overlapping pages. The table counts by unique report id.

## Next

- Decision 0001 and the triage page were last reviewed in June 2024. Matching on subject is
  what merged two causes; it needs a new decision record, not an edit.

